ta","data":[{"unit":1,"title":3,"area":74,"prev":76,"next":24,"titles":78,"firmware":248},{"id":2,"title":3,"aliases":4,"area":12,"order":13,"path":14,"context":15,"summary":18,"status":19,"firmware":20,"facts":25,"procedures":58,"parameters":59,"related":60,"body":64,"sources":65,"official_url":49,"controls":73},"com.midi-track-ccs","Track parameter CCs",[5,6,7,8,9,10,11],"cc lanes","lane model","parameter ccs","encoder ccs","filter cutoff cc","envelope cc","drum notes","com",75,"knowledge/manual/units/com/midi-track-ccs.md",{"modes":16,"screens":17},[],[],"Community charts and our lane model give each module page four consecutive CCs on the track's channel, one per encoder — `M1` on CC12–15 up to the LFO on CC40–43 — and put the 24 drum keys on notes 53–76.","unverified",{"min":21,"changed_in":22,"guide_version":23,"verified_on":24},"1.0.9",[],"1.1.15",null,[26,31,36,39,42,46,51,55],{"id":27,"text":28,"source":29,"firmware_min":24,"verified_on":24,"confidence":30},"lanes","Each module page answers four consecutive CCs on the track's channel, in encoder order `E1`…`E4`.","docs/research/20-midi-control.md#30-the-lane-model-derived","derived",{"id":32,"text":33,"source":34,"firmware_min":24,"verified_on":24,"confidence":35},"m1","The `M1` (engine) page uses CC12–15, whatever engine the track runs.","docs/research/20-midi-control.md#34-engine-resolved-names-for-cc1215-p1p4","community-verified",{"id":37,"text":38,"source":29,"firmware_min":24,"verified_on":24,"confidence":35},"m2","`M2` uses CC20–23 for the amp envelope (attack, decay, sustain, release), CC24–27 for the filter envelope and CC28–31 for its shift layer: play mode, portamento, bend range and preset volume.",{"id":40,"text":41,"source":29,"firmware_min":24,"verified_on":24,"confidence":35},"m3","`M3` uses CC32–35 for the filter (cutoff, resonance, envelope amount, key tracking) and CC36–39 for its shift layer, the sends to aux out, tape, FX I and FX II.",{"id":43,"text":44,"source":45,"firmware_min":24,"verified_on":24,"confidence":30},"m4","`M4` (the LFO) most likely uses CC40–43; community charts label these four CCs inconsistently.","docs/research/20-midi-control.md#37-known-conflicts--errata-in-the-sources",{"id":47,"text":48,"source":49,"firmware_min":24,"verified_on":24,"confidence":50},"mixer","The mixer controls sit outside the lanes, on CC7 (level), CC9 (mute) and CC10 (pan).","https://teenage.engineering/guides/op-xy/midi-references","official",{"id":52,"text":53,"source":54,"firmware_min":24,"verified_on":24,"confidence":35},"drum-notes","On drum tracks the 24 keys play MIDI notes 53–76 from left to right (F3–E5 when middle C is 60).","docs/research/20-midi-control.md#42-drum-key-mapping",{"id":56,"text":57,"source":54,"firmware_min":24,"verified_on":24,"confidence":35},"drum-kits","Which sound sits on which note depends on the loaded kit, so check the kit rather than assuming a General MIDI layout.",[],[],[61,62,63],"com.midi-cc-reference","instrument.engine-prism","com.controller-mode","The pattern — page by page, four CCs per page, left to right — comes from community charts and\nstored project data, not from TE, so treat it as a strong prediction. Filter cutoff on track 3 is\nCC32 on channel 3; CCs outside the listed ranges (such as CC16–19) are unconfirmed.",[66,68,69,70,72],{"url":29,"kind":67},"research",{"url":34,"kind":67},{"url":45,"kind":67},{"url":49,"kind":71},"guide",{"url":54,"kind":67},[],{"id":12,"title":75},"Connectivity",{"id":61,"title":77},"MIDI CC reference",{"basics.key-notation":79,"basics.main-modes":80,"basics.modules":81,"basics.track-buttons":82,"basics.linked-tracks":83,"basics.patterns-scenes-songs":84,"basics.workflow":85,"basics.firmware-versions":86,"hardware.layout":87,"hardware.power-and-charging":88,"hardware.connectors":89,"hardware.specifications":90,"hardware.te-boot":91,"hardware.firmware-update":92,"hardware.factory-reset":93,"hardware.system-menu":94,"hardware.safety-and-care":95,"hardware.in-the-box":96,"sequence